  "prompt": "Invent a short myth explaining why the sea is salty, in your own style.",
  "task_completions": [
    "Long ago, when the world was still cooling from the forge of creation, the Ocean was not a wild thing, but a vast, silver mirror of sweet, fresh water. In those days, the Moon was not a distant stone, but a lonely weaver who lived in a palace of pearl at the bottom of the deep.\n\nThe Moon-Weaver spent her eons spinning the tides, weaving the currents into intricate lace to keep the seabed tidy. She was content, but she possessed one great weakness: she was a collector of sorrows. \n\nWhenever a mortal on the surface suffered a heartbreak, or a lost sailor wept for a home he would never see, or a mother mourned a child taken by the wind, their tears did not simply vanish into the earth. They trickled down through the porous rock, filtering through the sand, until they gathered in the velvet halls of the Moon-Weaver.\n\nShe believed that if she gathered every tear ever shed, she could eventually weave them into a great, shimmering cloak of empathy—a garment that, if worn by the world, would end all suffering.\n\nFor ten thousand years, she stored the salt-water of human grief in great crystal amphorae. But the heart of humanity is a bottomless well, and the sorrows grew too heavy for the glass to hold. One night, during a particularly cruel winter of war and famine, the weight of the world’s misery became an unbearable tide. \n\nThe great amphorae shattered all at once.\n\nThe concentrated essence of a billion heartbreaks surged outward in a violent, white spray, flooding the sweet waters of the mirror-sea. The silver turned to turquoise, and the sweetness turned to brine.\n\nThe Moon-Weaver, devastated by the loss of her work, ascended to the sky, where she remains to this day. She still reaches down with her gravity, trying to pull the salt back toward her, tugging at the shores in a rhythmic, eternal longing. \n\nAnd that is why the sea is salty: it is the preserved memory of every tear the world has ever cried, held in the blue embrace of the deep.",
    "Long ago, when the world was still cooling from the forge of creation, the Sky and the Sea were deeply in love. The Sky was a restless spirit of sapphire and gold, and the Sea was a shimmering mirror of turquoise, forever reaching upward to touch his hem.\n\nThey lived in a state of eternal longing, for they could never truly embrace. Whenever the Sky leaned down to kiss the waves, he brought the storms; whenever the Sea rose up to meet him, she brought the fog.\n\nIn the center of the world lived the Weaver of Tears, a small, silver-skinned deity whose only purpose was to collect the sorrows of all living things. She kept these sorrows in a great, translucent urn, for she knew that if grief were left to wander the earth, it would poison the soil and wither the forests.\n\nOne eon, the Sky grew jealous of the land. He saw how the mountains could hold the earth and how the forests could cling to the hills. He wanted something to hold onto—something permanent. He begged the Weaver of Tears for a gift that would bind him to the Sea forever.\n\nThe Weaver, pitying the lonely Sky, agreed to help. But she had no gold or diamonds to give. She had only the urn of sorrows. \n\n\"I can give you a bond,\" the Weaver whispered, \"but it is a heavy one. It is the weight of every heartbreak, every lost love, and every silent goodbye ever felt by a creature of the earth.\"\n\nThe Sky, blinded by his longing, agreed. The Weaver tipped the great urn, pouring a cascading river of crystalline salt into the turquoise depths of the Sea.\n\nThe effect was instantaneous. The water grew heavy and dense, anchoring the Sea to the earth so she could no longer drift away. The salt acted as a cosmic glue, weaving the essence of the Sky’s longing into the very chemistry of the water. Now, whenever the Sky weeps in the form of rain, he is simply trying to wash away the bitterness of that ancient bargain.\n\nAnd so the sea remains salty—not from anger or malice, but because it carries the weight of every tear ever shed, holding the ocean in a permanent, aching embrace with the horizon.",
    "Long ago, when the world was still cooling from the forge of creation, the Ocean was not a wild thing, but a vast, silver mirror. He was a creature of absolute purity, transparent and sweet, reflecting the stars with such clarity that the Moon often leaned down to admire her own glow in his depths.\n\nIn those days, the Ocean was deeply in love with the Moon, but he was a prisoner of the shoreline, bound by the laws of the earth. He could only reach for her through the tides, stretching his liquid arms toward the sky in a desperate, rhythmic longing.\n\nThe Moon, though fond of the Ocean, was a wanderer of the void. She loved him, but she loved her freedom more. For eons, she danced just out of reach, her pale light teasing the surface of the water, promising a union that the laws of nature forbade.\n\nOne night, the Ocean could bear the distance no longer. He gathered all his strength and surged upward in a colossal, shimmering wave, attempting to leap from the earth and snatch the Moon from the velvet sky. He came so close that for one heartbeat, the spray of his crest brushed against her silver cheek.\n\nBut the gravity of the world is a jealous master. The Ocean was pulled back down with a violence that shook the seabed. As he crashed back into his basin, his heart—which had been pure, sweet water—shattered from the grief of the fall.\n\nThe Ocean did not die, for he was too vast to perish, but he was forever changed. His sweetness vanished, replaced by a bitter, stinging brine. Every drop of salt in the sea is a crystallized tear, a remnant of that one great heartbreak.\n\nTo this day, the Ocean continues to rise and fall, reaching for the Moon in an endless, rhythmic ache. And though he remains salt-bitter and restless, he still follows her wherever she goes, forever chasing the light that once almost touched him."
  ],
